From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: one model picker, and more AI providers

The model took two settings - a name to type, and a button that filled
it from the provider's list - and nothing said that an empty one turned
the feature off. It is one setting now: a button showing the model,
which opens the provider's own list, without the models that cannot
chat (speech, images, embeddings, live audio), and "Type a name..."
last. When the list cannot be read - no key yet, no network - it says
why and leaves the name to type. Changing the provider clears the
model, which was the other one's.

No model is marked or built in as suiting subtitles: such a list is out
of date within months, and a new release would need a new Kodi. Instead
the list starts with a "Newest" entry for each family the provider has
several releases of - "Newest gemini-flash-lite", now 3.5 - which names
the family, not the release: the family is the name without its version
("gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "qwen/qwen3.5-9b"
are gemini-flash-lite, gpt-mini, qwen/qwen-9b), and the release used is
the highest version, then the plain alias before its dated snapshots,
never a preview, an experiment or a "-latest" alias. It is found each
time the provider is asked whether it answers, in the background: a
subtitle opened before that goes without, rather than wait. The
provider's own "-latest" names come next, then every model. The list
and the newest are both taken from the models that can chat. A model
named that the provider no longer lists is replaced by the newest of
its family, and a notification says so when the replacement changes.

Google Gemini, Anthropic and NVIDIA join OpenRouter, OpenAI and a
server of one's own, at the OpenAI-style API each of them keeps, so
their address is not typed: Google's list names its models "models/..."
and puts its errors in a list, and Anthropic's answers only to its own
headers. A list in a shape other than OpenAI's, with no refusal in it,
lets the model named through.

Requests go without the range Kodi's HTTP client asks for: curl sends
it with a request's body as Content-Range, which Google refuses ("not
supported"); the others ignored it.

The reading of a model's answer, which already left out what it thought
first in <think>, now also leaves out <thought>, which Gemma writes, and
gives no answer when the reasoning was cut short. A refusal for the
provider's limit - HTTP 429, whoever the provider, for requests a minute
or a day or for credit - is said once a stream, naming the model, and
the lines after it are shown as they are. "Test the connection" says
how long the model took, and that it is no use for a film when it
reasoned first (in its answer or beside it) or took more than 15
seconds.

A "How to choose a provider" button, first in the section, opens a
guide: what is sent, which providers are free to try and how (as of
when it was written, saying that free offers change), which models
suit subtitles, and what Test the connection says.

The provider starts at None: nothing is sent anywhere until one is
chosen, and the key, model and test show only then. What uses it is
named for it - "AI translate subtitles", "AI repair" - and greyed out,
its help saying why, until a provider is chosen.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
